### Problem or Use Case

Hermes Agent is a personal AI assistant that runs per-user. It manages individual credentials, memory, conversations, and cron jobs tied to a single user's identity. Nix/Home Manager is the natural way to declare such user-level services on any Linux distro.

Currently, declarative setup for Hermes Agent is only supported on NixOS. There is not yet a declarative way to integrate it into a user’s Home Manager configuration.

### Proposed Solution

Provide a Home Manager module that allows users to declaratively configure Hermes Agent, e.g.:

```nix
programs.hermes-agent = {
  enable = true;
  package = pkgs.hermes-agent;  # or a flake input

  settings = {
    model = "claude-sonnet-4";
    provider = "anthropic";
  };
};
```



### Alternatives Considered

- NixOS system module: Hermes is inherently per-user, not per-system. A system-level module doesn't fit its use case (personal credentials, per-user memory).
- Manual `systemd --user` unit — Works but hard to manage.



### Feature Type

Configuration option

### Scope

Medium (few files, < 300 lines)

### Contribution

- [x] I'd like to implement this myself and submit a PR

### Bug Description

This is in `0.9.0` but may have been happening earlier, I just didn't notice. Permissions of new json conversation logs end up as 0600 so an ordinary user cannot write to them I guess. The save still occurs on `/exit`.

For example, run `hermes` (or `/new`) give it one prompt and then `/save`. Output `Failed to save: [Errno 13] Permission denied: 'hermes_conversation_20260413_220021.json'`.

This is running as a normal user, my NixOS config is:

```
services.hermes-agent = {
    enable = true;
    settings.model.default = <redacted>;
    environmentFiles = [ config.age.secrets.<redacted>.path ];
    addToSystemPackages = true;
    container = {
      enable = true;
      backend = "podman";
      hostUsers = [ "my-user" ];
    };
  };

  security.sudo.extraRules = [
    {
      users = [ "my-user" ];
      commands = [
        { command = "/run/current-system/sw/bin/podman"; options = [ "NOPASSWD" ]; }
      ];
    }
  ];
```

Claude is blaming this commit: https://github.com/NousResearch/hermes-agent/commit/f6857414
which seems to make sense, [`tempfile.mkstemp`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/tempfile.html#tempfile.mkstemp) is 600 by default and `os.replace` just keeps the same permissions.

### Steps to Reproduce

1. NixOS managed mode with above config
2. Run `hermes` as designated user
3. Say something, `/save`

### Expected Behavior

Successful save

### Actual Behavior

Permissions error

### Affected Component

CLI (interactive chat)

### Messaging Platform (if gateway-related)

_No response_

### Operating System

Nixos 25.11

### Python Version

3.13

### Hermes Version

0.9.0

### Relevant Logs / Traceback

```shell
`Failed to save: [Errno 13] Permission denied: 'hermes_conversation_20260413_220021.json'`
```

### Root Cause Analysis (optional)

`tempfile.mkstemp()` hardcodes mode 0600 for security and ignores umask. The
subsequent `os.replace()` preserves those bits. So every caller of
`atomic_json_write` produces 0600 files in a stateDir that's explicitly set
up (2770, setgid) to be group-shared.

### Proposed Fix (optional)

One line in `utils.py` after `os.replace(tmp_path, path)`:

```python
try:
    os.chmod(path, 0o666 & ~_get_umask())
except OSError:
    pass
```

where `_get_umask()` reads-and-restores via `os.umask(0); os.umask(prev)`.
That makes the final mode honor the process umask the way a plain `open()`
would have. For the NixOS managed-mode case where the service runs with
umask 0007, this yields 0660 — matching the directory's intent.

